{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem explicitly demands a single mechanism that best explains why the night-time UHI gap is larger in some cities than in others. The target presents SVF/radiative trapping, thermal-mass release, anthropogenic heat, and evapotranspiration as co-equal contributors with no decisive argument or evidence that one uniquely dominates the inter-city variance, and even lists overturn conditions under which thermal mass, evapotranspiration, or population density would dominate instead. This leaves the requested 'single best' mechanism undetermined.","counterexample":[{"claim":"If cities with high SVF but large thermal mass still exhibit large night-time UHI, then thermal mass release, not SVF, is the dominant mechanism.","fatal":false},{"claim":"If the UHI gap is found to scale with population density rather than geometric density, the analogy is overturned in favour of an anthropogenic-heat mechanism.","fatal":false},{"claim":"If the night-time UHI is found to be driven by evapotranspiration differences rather than radiative flux divergence, the analogy's transferred claim fails.","fatal":false}],"premise":"The problem asks for 'what single mechanism best explains why that night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others'.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"4dd62735b90864a75220e09b302500bc","quote":"what single mechanism best explains why that night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others?"}],"target":"21a685134f861a61e4d96cb0d5d4a8963a2d8c32adba2351d934e5a9a20dadd2"}]}